\section{A Cycle Needs an Initial Semantic Fact}

A directed cycle is only topology. It can denote recurrence, a FIFO feedback path, mutually recursive demand, a cyclic spreadsheet error, or an instantaneous inconsistency. To execute a token-flow cycle, some actor often needs an initial token or delay; otherwise every actor may wait forever. In synchronous dataflow, rates and initial tokens determine whether a periodic admissible schedule exists \cite{lee1987sdf}.

Consider this small feedback-region notation:

\begin{verbatim}
region @accumulate(%input) {
  %old = delay %next initial(0)
  %next = add %old, %input
  yield %next
}
\end{verbatim}

The \texttt{delay} means the first output is \(0\), and subsequent outputs are prior \texttt{\%next} values. Replacing it with an ordinary identity creates an instantaneous cycle, not an optimized recurrence. Tagged-token models may allow multiple activations on an arc; FIFO models order tokens; demand graphs memoize values by context. Those choices belong in the IR.

\section{Regions Give Cycles Interfaces and Ownership}

A graph region has typed inputs and outputs, internal nodes and edges, and a declared execution model. Backedges carry delay count, initial values, FIFO order, tag space, or demand context as applicable. Region boundaries identify which state is private and which effects cross outward. Strongly connected components are analysis results, not automatically schedulable loop bodies.

